import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.ClZupALV.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Contribute","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"howto/contribute.md","filePath":"howto/contribute.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"howto/contribute.md"};function l(o,e,r,d,h,c){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="Contribute" tabindex="-1">Contribute <a class="header-anchor" href="#Contribute" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Contribute {#Contribute}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>FFTL is meant to be developed by the Julia community, as the FFT the ecosystem shares. Help is most useful where one developer with two machines cannot reach:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Measurements on other hardware.</strong> FFTL is tuned on an Apple M1 Max, AMD EPYC (Zen 2 and 3), and an NVIDIA RTX A6000. Results on other CPUs and GPUs, and the cases where FFTL is slow or wrong there, show where the rules need work.</p></li><li><p><strong>Applications.</strong> A transform, number type or memory layout your code needs and FFTL does not offer.</p></li><li><p><strong>Kernels, tuning, documentation and tests.</strong></p></li></ul><p>Open an issue with the case (size, type, transform, machine, the numbers), or a pull request. Every change is checked against the tests, and every claim about speed against a measurement. This page shows how to set up, what to run, and what a change must come with. The reasons behind the design are on the <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR21/explanation/design-decisions#Design-decisions">Design decisions</a> page, and <code>AGENTS.md</code> in the repository lists the rules for contributors, human or not.</p><h2 id="Set-up" tabindex="-1">Set up <a class="header-anchor" href="#Set-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Set up {#Set-up}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> clone</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> https://github.com/JuliaMath/FFTL.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">cd</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> FFTL.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Work on a branch and open a pull request against <code>main</code>. Commit messages are in English and describe what the code does now, not how it got there.</p><h2 id="Run-the-tests" tabindex="-1">Run the tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#Run-the-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run the tests {#Run-the-tests}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> Pkg; Pkg</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">test</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;FFTL&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This runs a quick selection that covers every feature, in about 1.5 minutes. Two environment variables change it:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">variable</th><th style="text-align:left;">effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>FFTL_TEST_FULL=true</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the full lists of sizes, kinds and element types (weekly in CI)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>FFTL_TEST_VERBOSE=true</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the time of every test set</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The test files: <code>runtests.jl</code> (transforms against a reference DFT, both precisions, all kinds), <code>ka.jl</code> (KernelAbstractions plans, on the CPU backend), <code>inference.jl</code> (type inference of the public functions), <code>radix_table.jl</code> (the radix choice), and <code>aqua.jl</code> (package quality checks). CI runs the quick tests on Linux and macOS for pushes to <code>main</code> and for pull requests, and the full lists and Julia 1.10 once a week. The C library has its own CI job.</p><h2 id="Build-the-documentation" tabindex="-1">Build the documentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Build-the-documentation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build the documentation {#Build-the-documentation}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=docs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=docs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> docs/make.jl</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The output is in <code>docs/build</code>. Every Literate example in the getting started page and in the tutorials runs during the build, so a broken example fails the build.</p><h2 id="Changes-to-performance" tabindex="-1">Changes to performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Changes-to-performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changes to performance {#Changes-to-performance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A change that claims to be faster comes with an A/B measurement on the same machine, in one process per variant, with the same inputs. Report the medians of several interleaved runs and the spread between two runs of the same code: a difference inside that spread is no difference. The <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR21/explanation/performance#Performance">Performance</a> page describes the protocol.</p><h2 id="Where-things-are" tabindex="-1">Where things are <a class="header-anchor" href="#Where-things-are" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where things are {#Where-things-are}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">file</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/codegen.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the symbolic generator of the straight-line codelets</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/kernels.jl</code>, <code>src/complexvec.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">codelets on SIMD vectors, the complex arithmetic</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/factor.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the radix sequence chosen for a length</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/plan.jl</code>, <code>src/rchain.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Stockham chains and Bluestein&#39;s algorithm; <code>rchain.jl</code> is the form that libfftl compiles</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/kernel_methods.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the stage kernels and short chains of the fixed codelet set as plain methods (see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR21/explanation/image-size#Package-size-and-load-time">Package size and load time</a>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/threads.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">threads, lane blocks, the four-step algorithm</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/interface.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the complex plans and the public functions</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/real.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">real transforms (r2c, c2r)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/r2r.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the real-to-real kinds</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/rreal.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">run-time real plans, the form that libfftl compiles</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/generic.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the path for other number types</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/ka.jl</code>, <code>ext/</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">GPU through KernelAbstractions</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/precompile.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the precompile workload</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>src/limits.jl</code>, <code>src/tuning.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">cache sizes and thresholds</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>libfftl/</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the C library: FFTW3&#39;s C and Fortran interfaces over the run-time plans</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The thresholds in <code>src/tuning.jl</code> are numbers measured on two machines. A change to them needs a measurement on at least one machine, reported with the change.</p><h2 id="Licensing" tabindex="-1">Licensing <a class="header-anchor" href="#Licensing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Licensing {#Licensing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>FFTL is MIT-licensed. Code in a contribution must be your own or come from a source under a permissive license (MIT, BSD, Apache), with the notice kept. Do not copy or translate code from GPL-licensed FFT implementations, such as FFTW or KFR. Algorithms published in papers can be implemented from the paper.</p>`,22)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",l]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
